# ---------------------------------------------------------------- Greek and Latin fathers
add(id="basil-hex-2-8", work="basil-hex-lat", author="basil", tradition="greek",
    date=380, date_precision="range-370-380", place="caesarea-cappadociae",
    anchor={"verse": "gen.1.4"}, lemma={"la": "incommixtam naturam", "en": "their nature unmingled"},
    original=latin("7608", "Et separavit Deus inter lucem et tenebras, id est incommixtam",
                   "Et vocavit Deus lucem diem, et tenebras vocavit noctem.", 53),
    english={"text": "And God separated between the light and the darkness — that is, he fashioned their nature unmingled, and unlike itself by contrariety; for he both set them apart from one another by long spaces and distinguished them, as it is said: And God called the light day, and the darkness he called night.", **APPROVED},
    cruxes=["good-and-separated"], senses=["literal"],
    answers=["divisio-loci"],
    notes="The Greek bench's answer, in the Latin Eustathius made of it, and it is entirely physical: the division is a fashioning of the two natures so that they cannot mix, and a placing of them at long distances from each other. What Basil will not do is make the darkness nothing. He has just said, a few columns earlier, that night is the shadowing of the air which the sun's setting produces; here the darkness is a nature with a contrary, and the separating is a work on both. Bede's 'not only by a difference of quality but of place' is this sentence in another language, and Bede did not need to be reading it to arrive there — but Angelomus, who did read something like it, attributes it to Clement.")

add(id="ambrose-hex-1-9b", work="ambrose-hex", author="ambrose", tradition="latin",
    date=387, date_precision="circa", place="milan",
    anchor={"verse": "gen.1.4"}, lemma={"la": "nihil videatur intra se habere confusum", "en": "nothing may be seen to have anything confused within it"},
    original=latin("6958", "Et discrevit inter lucem et tenebras; et vidit Deus lucem quia bona est",
                   "nihil videatur intra se habere confusum.", 14),
    english={"text": "And he distinguished between the light and the darkness; and God saw the light, that it was good (Gen 1:4). He spoke, and no one heard the sound of a voice; he distinguished, and no one caught the labour of the working; he saw, and no one beheld the straining of his eyes. And God saw, it says, the light, that it was good. He did not see what he did not know; nor did he approve what he had not known before, or had not seen: but it is the property of good works that they need no one else to commend them, but attest their own grace when they are seen. What is proved by the sight is more than what is praised by speech. For it uses its own testimony, not another's suffrage. And if among us judgement is issued through the eyes, by which both the grace of beauty and the measure of things is grasped at once, how much more does God see all that he approves, and approve what he sees, according to what is written: The eyes of the Lord are upon the just (Ps 34:16 [Vg 33:16])? Such is the nature of light, that its grace lies not in number, not in measure, not in weight, as other things do, but wholly in the beholding. In its own words, then, he expressed the nature of light, which pleases by being seen, since it is light itself that supplies the office of seeing. Nor was it without cause that it could find so great a herald, by whom it is rightly praised first, since it is light that made the other members of the world worthy of praise too. God therefore saw the light, and lit it with his own face, and saw that it was good. This is not a judgement on God's part, but a general one. And so the grace of light is proved not only in its splendour but in its whole usefulness. And hence the distinction is made between the light and the darkness, so that, the nature of light and of darkness being separated, nothing may be seen to have anything confused within it.", **APPROVED},
    cruxes=["good-and-separated"], senses=["literal"],
    answers=["bonum-approbatio", "divisio-loci"],
    notes="The one witness on either bench who takes 'and God saw that it was good' as a statement about the nature of light rather than about the nature of God's knowledge. Ambrose's problem is not how God can be said to see; he disposes of that in a clause — he did not see what he did not know. His interest is that light is the one thing whose whole grace is in the beholding, so that it is the only creature that could have been praised first, and that a good work needs no external commender but attests itself when it is seen. Only then does he reach the division, and it comes out in a single purpose clause: so that nothing may be seen to have anything confused within it. That is Rashi's plain sense at Gen 1:4 — that it was not seemly that light and darkness should function in confusion — in Milan, and neither could have known the other.")

add(id="aug-gnm-1-9", work="aug-gnm", author="augustine", tradition="latin",
    date=389, date_precision="range-388-390", place="thagaste",
    anchor={"verse": "gen.1.4"}, lemma={"la": "tenebrae lucis absentia est", "en": "darkness is the absence of light"},
    original=latin("7303", "Et divisit Deus inter lucem et tenebras, et vocavit Deus diem lucem",
                   "vel non dat, vel detrahit.", 34),
    english={"text": "And God divided between the light and the darkness, and God called the light day, and the darkness he called night. It is not said here, God made the darkness; because the darkness, as was said above, is the absence of light: yet a distinction was made between light and darkness. Just as we make a voice by crying out, but make silence by not sounding, because the ceasing of the voice is silence — yet we distinguish by a kind of sense between voice and silence, and call the one voice and the other silence: as therefore we are rightly said to make silence, so in many places of the divine Scriptures God is rightly said to make darkness, because he either does not give light, or withdraws it, at what times and in what places he wills.", **APPROVED},
    cruxes=["good-and-separated"], senses=["literal"],
    answers=["tenebrae-privatio", "divisio-est-ordinatio"],
    notes="The sentence that decided the question for eight hundred years, and the analogy that carried it. Scripture does not say God made the darkness, because darkness is the absence of light; but a distinction was nevertheless made — as we make a voice by crying out and make silence by not sounding, and yet distinguish the two and name them. The care of the argument is in the last clause, which concedes the whole difficulty: Scripture does in many places say that God makes darkness, and Augustine will not deny it, so he redefines the making — God makes darkness by not giving light, or by taking it away, where and when he chooses. Angelomus copies the analogy verbatim without the concession and with 'gives or withdraws' in place of 'does not give or withdraws'; Wigbod's compiler puts the opposite doctrine into Augustine's mouth on the same page.")

add(id="aug-gnl-imp-5-23", work="aug-gnl-imp", author="augustine", tradition="latin",
    date=393, date_precision="circa", place="hippo",
    anchor={"verse": "gen.1.4"}, lemma={"la": "quae enim societas lucis cum tenebris", "en": "for what fellowship has light with darkness?"},
    original=latin("7310", "Et divisit Deus inter lucem et tenebras. Hinc intelligi licet",
                   "aut plenum et inane, et similia.", 34),
    english={"text": "And God divided between the light and the darkness. From this it may be understood with how great an ease of divine working these things are said to have been effected. For there is no one who so supposes the light to have been made that it was confused with the darkness, and for that reason needed a separation afterwards; but by the very fact that the light was made, the division between light and darkness also followed. For what fellowship has light with darkness (2 Cor 6:14)? God therefore divided between light and darkness, because he made the light, whose absence is called darkness. And there is a difference between light and darkness such as there is between a garment and nakedness, or the full and the empty, and the like.", **APPROVED},
    cruxes=["good-and-separated"], senses=["literal"],
    answers=["tenebrae-privatio", "divisio-est-instantanea"],
    notes="The objection Augustine is answering is never stated on the rabbinic bench and is the obvious one: if God had to divide them, they must first have been mixed. His answer is that no separate act is needed — the division followed on the making, because the making of light is the not-being of darkness, as putting on a garment is the ending of nakedness and filling a vessel the ending of its emptiness. Hugh of St Victor takes over the instantaneity and applies it to the angels; and the same instinct, that the two were never confused and needed no unmixing, is what Ambrose gets from the other end when he says the division exists so that nothing should have anything confused within it.")

add(id="aug-gnl-imp-5-25", work="aug-gnl-imp", author="augustine", tradition="latin",
    date=393, date_precision="circa", place="hippo",
    anchor={"verse": "gen.1.4"}, lemma={"la": "quaedam et facit Deus, et ordinat; quaedam vero tantum ordinat", "en": "some things God both makes and orders; some he only orders"},
    original=latin("7310", "Sive ergo lucem aetheream, sive sensualem",
                   "ipso ordinante pulchra sunt omnia.", 34),
    english={"text": "Whether, then, this sentence would have us understand the ethereal light, or the sensitive light in which animals share, or the rational light which angels and men have, to have been made by God first in the nature of things — that God divided between light and darkness, by the very fact that light was made, must be taken to mean that light is one thing and those privations of light another, which God set in order under the name of their contrary, darkness. For it is not said that God made the darkness: since God made the forms themselves, not the privations, which belong to nothing, whence all things were made by God the craftsman; yet we understand that they were set in order by him, when it is said, And God divided between light and darkness — lest even those privations should not have their own order, God ruling and administering all things. As in singing, the interpositions of silences at fixed and measured intervals, although they are privations of sound, are nevertheless well ordered by those who know how to sing, and contribute something to the sweetness of the whole song. And the shadows in paintings set off whatever is more prominent, and please not by their form but by their order. For God is not the author of our vices either; yet he is their orderer, when he sets sinners in that place and compels them to suffer what they deserve. To this belongs it that the sheep are set on the right hand and the goats on the left (Matt 25:33). Some things, then, God both makes and orders; some he only orders. The just he both makes and orders; sinners, in so far as they are sinners, he does not make but only orders. Since when he sets these on the right and those on the left, and bids them go into eternal fire, it belongs to the order of their deserts. So he both makes and orders the forms and natures themselves; but the privations of forms and the defects of natures he does not make, but only orders. And so he said, Let there be light; and light was made. He did not say, Let there be darkness; and darkness was made. Of these, then, he made one and did not make the other, yet ordered both, when God divided between light and darkness. And so, by his making, the several things are beautiful; and by his ordering, all things are beautiful.", **APPROVED},
    cruxes=["good-and-separated"], senses=["literal", "allegorical"],
    answers=["tenebrae-privatio", "divisio-est-ordinatio", "tenebrae-peccatores"],
    notes="The best thing said on this crux in Latin, and the one place where Augustine's privation doctrine stops being a piece of metaphysics and becomes an aesthetic. God did not make the darkness; but he ordered it, and the dividing is the ordering — 'lest even those privations should not have their own order'. Then the two analogies, which are not decoration but the argument: the rests in a song, which are privations of sound and are nevertheless placed at measured intervals by anyone who knows how to sing and add to the sweetness of the whole; and the shadows in a painting, which set off what is prominent and 'please not by their form but by their order'. And then the step that makes the reading moral without making the darkness a substance: God is not the author of our vices, but he is their orderer, when he puts sinners in the place they deserve — sheep on the right and goats on the left. The rabbinic bench's moral reading of this verse (R. Yanai, R. Abahu) reaches the same pairing of righteous and wicked at the same clause and never has to make the darkness nothing to do it, because it never took up the question of whether darkness is a thing.")

add(id="aug-civ-11-19", work="aug-civ", author="augustine", tradition="latin",
    date=417, date_precision="range-413-427", place="hippo",
    anchor={"verse": "gen.1.4"}, lemma={"la": "inter sanctos Angelos et immundos fuisse discretum", "en": "that a distinction was made between the holy angels and the unclean"},
    original=latin("21364", "Quamvis itaque divini sermonis obscuritas",
                   "occultum aut incertum esse non potuit.", 41),
    english={"text": "Although, then, the obscurity of the divine speech is useful even for this, that it brings forth many opinions of the truth and produces them into the light of knowledge, while one man understands it this way and another that — provided always that what is understood in an obscure place is confirmed either by the attestation of manifest things, or by other passages not at all doubtful; and provided that, when many things are handled, one arrives also at what the writer meant, or, if that lies hidden, that on occasion of handling a deep obscurity some other true things are said: it does not seem to me an opinion at odds with the works of God, if, when that first light was made, the angels are understood to have been created, and a distinction to have been made between the holy angels and the unclean, where it is said, And God divided between the light and the darkness; and God called the light day, and the darkness he called night. For he alone could distinguish these, who could also foreknow, before they fell, that they would fall, and that, deprived of the light of truth, they would remain in darksome pride. For between this day and night best known to us — that is, between this light and these darknesses — he commanded the luminaries of heaven most familiar to our senses to divide: Let there be, he said, luminaries in the firmament of heaven, that they may shine upon the earth, and divide between the day and the night. And a little after: And God made, he says, two great luminaries; the greater luminary for the beginning of the day, and the lesser luminary for the beginning of the night; and the stars: and God set them in the firmament of heaven, to shine upon the earth, and to preside over the day and the night, and to divide between the light and the darkness (Gen 1:4, 5, 14, 16–18). But between that light, which is the holy fellowship of the angels shining intelligibly with the illumination of truth, and the darkness contrary to it — that is, the most foul minds of the evil angels turned away from the light of justice — he alone could divide, to whom even a future evil, not of nature but of will, could not be hidden or uncertain.", **APPROVED},
    cruxes=["good-and-separated"], senses=["allegorical"],
    answers=["tenebrae-angeli", "divisio-est-judicium"],
    notes="Augustine's angelic reading, and it is offered with more hedging than anything else he says about this verse: the obscurity of Scripture is useful because it breeds many true readings, and 'it does not seem to me an opinion at odds with the works of God' if the light is the angels and the division is between the holy and the unclean. The argument for it is a philological one — the luminaries of the fourth day are commanded to divide light from darkness in the ordinary sense, so the division of the first day must be another division — and it depends on nobody but God being able to make it, since it separates by foreknowledge of a fall that has not happened. This is the reading Wigbod, Angelomus, Hugh, Rupert and Comestor all take up, and none of them keeps the hedge.")

add(id="aug-civ-11-20", work="aug-civ", author="augustine", tradition="latin",
    date=417, date_precision="range-413-427", place="hippo",
    anchor={"verse": "gen.1.4"}, lemma={"la": "ne utrumque appellaretur bonum", "en": "lest both should be called good"},
    original=latin("21364", "Denique nec illud est praetereundum silentio",
                   "non tamen fuerant approbandae.", 41),
    english={"text": "And this too is not to be passed over in silence, that where God said, Let there be light, and light was made, there was at once added, And God saw the light, that it was good — not after he had separated between the light and the darkness, and called the light day and the darkness night, lest he should seem to have borne the testimony of his approval to such darkness along with the light. For where the darkness is blameless, between which and this light visible to our eyes the luminaries of heaven divide, it is put not before but after: And God saw that it was good. And he set them, it says, in the firmament of heaven, to shine upon the earth, and to preside over the day and the night, and to separate between the light and the darkness. And God saw that it was good. For both pleased him, because both are without sin. But where God said, Let there be light, and light was made, and God saw the light, that it was good; and afterwards it is added, And God separated between the light and the darkness; and God called the light day, and the darkness he called night (Gen 1:3–5, 17, 18) — in this place it is not added, And God saw that it was good; lest both should be called good, when one of them was evil, by its own vice and not by nature. And therefore the light alone pleased the Creator there: but the angelic darkness, though it had to be ordered, did not have to be approved.", **APPROVED},
    cruxes=["good-and-separated"], senses=["allegorical"],
    answers=["bonum-withheld", "tenebrae-angeli"],
    notes="The star witness of this crux, and the argument is made entirely out of where a clause stands. On the first day the approval comes immediately after the making and before the division; on the fourth day, where the darkness is blameless, it comes after the division. So the placing is deliberate: on day one God does not say 'it was good' after separating light from darkness, lest he seem to approve such darkness along with the light. The reason is given in five words — 'lest both should be called good, when one of them was evil' — and the last sentence keeps the distinction the whole Augustinian doctrine rests on: the angelic darkness had to be ordered, but did not have to be approved. Bereshit Rabbah 3:6 reads the same silence out of the next verse by exactly the same method — 'and to the darkness God called night' is not written here, but 'and to the darkness he called night' — and R. Abahu at 2:5 argues the identical point from the identical omission at the identical clause.")

add(id="isidore-quaest-1-4", work="isidore-quaest", author="isidore", tradition="latin",
    date=630, date_precision="circa", place="seville",
    anchor={"verse": "gen.1.4"}, lemma={"la": "divisit justos a peccatoribus, tanquam a tenebris", "en": "he divided the just from sinners, as from darkness"},
    original=latin("21433", "Et divisit lucem a tenebris; nam tunc Deus",
                   "nunc autem lux in Domino (Ephes. V, 8) .", 83),
    english={"text": "And he divided the light from the darkness; for then God, according to the favour of his foreknowledge, divided the just — that is, the sons of God and of light — from sinners, as from darkness; calling the just day, and those others night. For that in the Church the just are called by the name of light, hear the Apostle: You were once, he says, darkness, but now light in the Lord (Eph 5:8).", **APPROVED},
    cruxes=["good-and-separated"], senses=["allegorical"],
    answers=["tenebrae-peccatores", "divisio-est-judicium"],
    notes="Isidore takes the verse straight into the Church, and the sequence he is running is a reading of the six days as the six ages of the Christian life, so that the division of light from darkness is the first thing that happens to a believer and the firmament on the second day is the solidity of Scripture. What matters for this crux is that the division is God's foreknowledge sorting the just from sinners: the same act Augustine locates among the angels, moved down to human beings, with Eph 5:8 doing for Isidore what Ps 75 does for Rupert. The rabbinic bench reaches the identical pairing at the identical clause, and reaches it by reading the darkness as the deeds of the wicked rather than the wicked themselves — a difference that runs through every allegory on both benches and is never once discussed.")

add(id="bede-gen-1-4", work="bede-gen", author="bede", tradition="latin",
    date=720, date_precision="range-703-725", place="jarrow",
    anchor={"verse": "gen.1.4"}, lemma={"la": "non solum qualitatis, sed et locorum distantia", "en": "not only by a difference of quality, but of places"},
    original=latin("8466", "Et divisit lucem ac teneoras. Divisit namque eas",
                   "priscis in tenebris remanere sinendo.", 91),
    english={"text": "And he divided the light and the darkness. For he divided them not only by a difference of quality, but of places: namely by spreading the light over the upper part of the world, in which the human way of life was to be, and by allowing the lower parts of it to remain in their ancient darkness.", **APPROVED},
    cruxes=["good-and-separated"], senses=["literal"],
    answers=["divisio-loci"],
    notes="Two clauses, and they set the Latin bench's literal answer for the next five hundred years: the division is by quality and by place, the light spread over the upper part of the world where men were going to live and the lower part left in the darkness it already had. Bede does not argue with Augustine; he simply does not raise the question whether darkness is a thing, and answers a question about where. The sentence is copied verbatim by Rabanus, taken over by Angelomus with an attribution to Clement, and reappears in Comestor as 'he divided the created things by distance of places and by quality'. It is also, without any contact, the same answer as Basil's long distances and the same answer as R. Yoḥanan and Resh Lakish's two generals, each given his bound.")

add(id="wigbod-gen-1-4", work="wigbod-gen", author="wigbod", tradition="latin",
    date=790, date_precision="circa", place="tours",
    anchor={"verse": "gen.1.4"}, lemma={"la": "sicut est essentia lucis, ita essentia tenebrarum", "en": "as there is an essence of light, so an essence of darkness"},
    original=latin("8606", "AUGUSTINUS. D. Et divisit lucem a tenebris",
                   "Id est, distinctio rei formatae ab informi.", 96),
    english={"text": "AUGUSTINE. D[isciple]. And he divided the light from the darkness. Scripture says he divided the light from the darkness: for everything that is divided can also be. As therefore there is an essence of light, so there is an essence of darkness. And why does Scripture not say, God saw the darkness, that it was good, as was said of the light? M[aster]. God with a good and unchangeable will created all the angels good, but because he foreknew that some of them would fall through pride, by the unchangeableness of his foreknowledge he divided between the good and the evil. Calling the evil darkness, then, and the good light, he says of these that they are good, and of those he says it not at all, lest he should seem to approve the sins which were the darkness. ELSEWHERE AGAIN. D. He divided the light from the darkness. What is it to divide the light from the darkness? M. It is the distinction of the formed thing from the unformed.", **APPROVED},
    cruxes=["good-and-separated"], senses=["literal", "allegorical"],
    answers=["tenebrae-creatura", "bonum-withheld", "formatum-ab-informi"],
    notes="A Carolingian compilation putting under the rubric AUGUSTINUS the one proposition Augustine spent three works denying. The pupil's inference is a good one and it is not Augustine's: everything that is divided can also be, therefore as there is an essence of light so there is an essence of darkness. The pupil then asks the right question — why does Scripture not say God saw the darkness that it was good? — and the master's answer is Augustine's own from City of God XI.20, almost word for word, down to 'lest he should seem to approve'. So the transmission carries the answer and loses the doctrine it was an answer to: within two sentences the darkness has an essence and is being withheld from approval because it is sin. The third exchange then gives a third answer, the distinction of the formed from the unformed, which is Honorius' and Abelard's and belongs to a different question again.")

add(id="rabanus-gen-1-4", work="rabanus-gen", author="rabanus", tradition="latin",
    date=825, date_precision="range-820-830", place="fulda",
    anchor={"verse": "gen.1.4"}, lemma={"la": "non solum qualitatis, sed et locorum distantia", "en": "not only by a difference of quality, but of places"},
    original=latin("8885", "Et divisit lucem ac tenebras. Divisit namque eas",
                   "priscis in tenebris remanere sinendo.", 107),
    english={"text": "And he divided the light and the darkness. For he divided them not only by a difference of quality, but of places: namely by spreading the light over the upper part of the world, in which the human way of life was to be, and by allowing the lower parts of it to remain in their ancient darkness.", **APPROVED},
    cruxes=["good-and-separated"], senses=["literal"],
    answers=["divisio-loci"],
    notes="Bede's two clauses reproduced without a word changed and without a name — the commonest event on the Carolingian bench and worth one card, because it is what makes the difference of quality and of place the standing Latin answer rather than one Northumbrian's opinion. Rabanus is compiling a commentary for the schools out of the fathers, and Bede is by now among the fathers.")

add(id="angelom-gen-1-4", work="angelom-gen", author="angelomus", tradition="latin",
    date=850, date_precision="circa", place="luxeuil",
    anchor={"verse": "gen.1.4"}, lemma={"la": "distinctio facta est inter lucem et tenebras, non tenebrae factae", "en": "a distinction was made between light and darkness, not darkness made"},
    original=latin("9032", "Caeterum dum dicit, divisitque lucem a tenebris",
                   "aeterna facta est stabilitas permanentium", 115),
    english={"text": "But when he says, and he divided the light from the darkness, he shows that a distinction was made between light and darkness, not that darkness was made; as we say we make a voice by crying out, and silence by not crying out, because the ceasing of the voice is silence. Yet we distinguish between them by a kind of sense, and call the one voice and the other silence. As therefore we rightly say that we make silence, so God is rightly said to make darkness; because he either gives or withdraws light, at what times and places he wills. Because, as Clement says: Lighting up one part of the world, he left the other dark — namely by spreading the light over the upper part of the world, in which the human way of life was to be, and the lower parts of it remaining in their ancient darkness. If anyone chooses to take it so, we, as we began, have striven to follow blessed Augustine and the exposition of the master. And God divided the light and the darkness, and the rest. That is, the heavenly citizens of the angels from the unclean spirits, when Lucifer, turning away from the Creator and turning to himself, reckoning that he sufficed to himself, swollen with pride, was cast out from the heavenly places with his followers, without the help of his maker, and the rest remained in their stability — calling these day and those night, according to what follows: and he called the light day, and the darkness night. For the ruin of those who fell became the eternal stability of those who remained.", **APPROVED},
    cruxes=["good-and-separated"], senses=["literal", "allegorical"],
    answers=["tenebrae-privatio", "divisio-loci", "tenebrae-angeli"],
    notes="Three answers laid end to end with a hinge sentence between them, and the hinge is the most honest thing in the Carolingian bench: 'if anyone chooses to take it so, we, as we began, have striven to follow blessed Augustine.' First Augustine's privation with the silence analogy, copied close enough to be recognised and altered in one word — Augustine wrote that God either does not give light or withdraws it, Angelomus writes that he gives or withdraws it, which quietly makes the darkness something conferred. Then Bede's upper and lower parts of the world, attributed to Clement. Then the angelic reading, with Lucifer named and the fall narrated, and a sentence that is not in Augustine at all: the ruin of those who fell became the eternal stability of those who remained. A compiler who cannot choose is not thereby a bad witness; the three answers are the three the twelfth century will still be sorting.")

add(id="remigius-gen-1-4", work="remigius-gen", author="remigius", tradition="latin",
    date=890, date_precision="circa", place="auxerre",
    anchor={"verse": "gen.1.4"}, lemma={"la": "utrumque autem fecit Deus et lucem et tenebras", "en": "but God made both, the light and the darkness"},
    original=latin("9346", "Et divisit lucem a tenebris. Tenebrae a tenendo",
                   "etiam tenebrae cum luce ponuntur.", 131),
    english={"text": "And he divided the light from the darkness. Darkness (tenebrae) is so called from holding (tenendo), because it holds the eyes so that they may not see. But God made both, the light and the darkness — namely, that men might work in the light and rest in the night. Therefore the darkness too is a work of God. Whence in the hymn of the three young men, in which every creature is invited to bless God, the darkness also is set down along with the light.", **APPROVED},
    cruxes=["good-and-separated"], senses=["literal"],
    answers=["tenebrae-creatura", "night-for-rest"],
    notes="The flat contradiction of Augustine, stated in four sentences and with a proof text, in a school commentary written for teaching. God made both, and the reason is a reason from use: that men should work in the light and rest at night. Then the sentence Augustine's whole argument exists to prevent — therefore the darkness too is a work of God — and then the evidence, which is liturgical rather than philosophical: in the Benedicite, where every creature is called to bless the Lord, light and darkness are called together (Dan 3:72). It is a good argument. If the darkness is a privation it cannot be summoned to praise, and the canticle summons it. Remigius does not mention Augustine, and the Glossa will print this gloss over the name of Strabo on the same page as glosses that assume the opposite.")

add(id="remigius-gen-1-4b", work="remigius-gen", author="remigius", tradition="latin",
    date=890, date_precision="circa", place="auxerre",
    anchor={"verse": "gen.1.4"}, lemma={"la": "filii Seth separati sunt ab impia generatione Cain", "en": "the sons of Seth were separated from the impious generation of Cain"},
    original=latin("9346", "Divisit lucem a tenebris in hac die",
                   "ut induceret Deus diluvium.", 131),
    english={"text": "He divided the light from the darkness on this day: because in this age the sons of God, that is, the sons of Seth, were separated from the impious generation of Cain, whose evening was the multiplied iniquity of men, that God might bring on the flood.", **APPROVED},
    cruxes=["good-and-separated"], senses=["allegorical"],
    answers=["tenebrae-peccatores", "divisio-est-judicium"],
    notes="The same commentator, the same verse, and a wholly different reading a few columns later, because Remigius runs the six days a second time as the six ages of the world. On the second pass the division of light from darkness is a datable event in Genesis: the separation of the sons of Seth from the line of Cain, with the multiplied iniquity of men as its evening and the flood following. It is the most concrete form the moral reading takes anywhere on the Latin bench — not the just in general, but two named genealogies — and it is exactly the move Bereshit Rabbah does not make, since for R. Yanai and R. Abahu the light and the darkness are deeds and not families.")

add(id="alcuin-int-35", work="alcuin-int", author="alcuin", tradition="latin",
    date=800, date_precision="circa", place="tours",
    anchor={"verse": "gen.1.4"}, lemma={"la": "in ea bonitate placuit ut maneret, in qua placuit ut fieret", "en": "it pleased him to remain in that goodness in which it pleased him to be made"},
    original=latin("21416", "Inter. 35. Quid est: Vidit Deus, quod esset bonum",
                   "in qua placuit ut fieret, quod fecit.", 100),
    english={"text": "Question 35. What is: God saw that it was good? — Answer. That is: what he made pleased him to remain in that goodness in which it pleased him to be made.", **APPROVED},
    cruxes=["good-and-separated"], senses=["literal"],
    answers=["bonum-approbatio"],
    notes="One sentence, and it removes the difficulty the whole Latin bench is worrying at. If 'God saw that it was good' were an inspection, God would be learning something; Alcuin makes it a persistence of the same pleasure — what pleased him to be made pleased him to remain. Augustine puts the same thing at City of God XI.21 with more machinery ('he teaches that it is good, he does not learn it') and Comestor will divide the moment in two, pleasing in foreknowledge that it should be made and pleasing in being that it should stay. The Carolingian schoolbook has it in twenty words and does not notice that it is an answer to anything.")

add(id="glossa-1-4", work="glossa", author="glossa-ordinaria", tradition="latin",
    date=1120, date_precision="compilation-1110-1130", place="laon",
    anchor={"verse": "gen.1.4"}, lemma={"la": "Lucem et tenebras fecit Deus", "en": "God made both the light and the darkness"},
    original=latin("8950", "VERS. 4.-- « Et vidit Deus lucem, » etc. (STRAB.)",
                   "propter quam fidem Dominus apparere dignatus est.", 113),
    english={"text": "VERSE 4. 'And God saw the light,' etc. (STRABO.) God made both the light and the darkness, whence Daniel 3: 'Bless the Lord, light and darkness,' since every creature is invited to bless. (ISIDORE, on Genesis, ch. 2.) Everyone has in his works and upright life six distinct days, after which he may hope for rest: on the first day, the light of faith, since he first believes in things invisible, for which faith the Lord deigned to appear.", **APPROVED},
    cruxes=["good-and-separated"], senses=["literal", "allegorical"],
    answers=["tenebrae-creatura", "tenebrae-peccatores"],
    notes="The whole of the standard gloss on this verse is two sentences, and what they select is the answer to the crux. Of everything the Latin bench had said about Gen 1:4 — Augustine's privation, his angels, his order without making, Bede's places, Ambrose on the nature of light — the Glossa prints Remigius' 'God made both the light and the darkness' with the Benedicite behind it, and Isidore's light of faith. So the twelfth-century schoolroom's default reading of this verse is the anti-Augustinian one on the literal side and the moral one on the allegorical, and Augustine's answer, which every twelfth-century witness on this crux still argues from, has to be fetched from elsewhere. Note also that the gloss on 1:2 in the same chapter carries the opposite doctrine — 'darkness was there, namely the privation of true light' — three columns earlier.")

# ---------------------------------------------------------------- the twelfth century
add(id="abelard-hex-1-4", work="abelard-hex", author="abelard", tradition="latin",
    date=1133, date_precision="circa", place="paraclete",
    anchor={"verse": "gen.1.4"}, lemma={"la": "perfectum discrevit ab imperfecto et distinctum a confuso", "en": "he distinguished the perfect from the imperfect and the distinct from the confused"},
    original=latin("11118", "Et divisit lucem a tenebris. Hoc est operatione sua perfectum",
                   "quam de operum formatione tribuatur.", 178),
    english={"text": "And he divided the light from the darkness. That is, by his working he distinguished the perfect from the imperfect and the distinct from the confused. Above, where he says, And God said: Let there be light, and light was made, he showed that the light was made at God's command. But here, when he says, And he divided the light from the darkness, he shows that this distinction of works also, which he calls light, was made by him; so that one and the same is understood to be both the Creator of the matter and the former of it, and the whole praise, both for the creation of the matter and for the forming of the works, is attributed to him.", **APPROVED},
    cruxes=["good-and-separated"], senses=["literal"],
    answers=["formatum-ab-informi", "divisio-est-ordinatio"],
    notes="Abelard makes the verse do a piece of work nobody else asks of it. The light is the distinction of the works and the darkness is the confusion out of which they were distinguished — so that Gen 1:3 attributes the creating of matter to God and Gen 1:4 attributes the forming of it to the same God, and the whole praise falls in one place. The point is directed against a reading in which matter is one thing and its forming another, and it is the argument he needed at Gen 1:1 (see K4); here he gets it out of the dividing. It also means that on this crux the darkness is neither a privation nor an element nor an angel, but a state of the world before it was ordered — which is Honorius' and Wigbod's third answer as well, arrived at from the other end.")

add(id="hugh-sacr-1-4", work="hugh-sacr", author="hugh-of-st-victor", tradition="latin",
    date=1134, date_precision="range-1130-1137", place="paris",
    anchor={"verse": "gen.1.4"}, lemma={"la": "divisit per judicium; et per meritum nominavit", "en": "he divided by judgement, and named by merit"},
    original=latin("11082", "Et ut mihi videtur eodem prorsus temporis momento",
                   "vocavit lucem diem, et tenebras noctem.", 176, occurrence=0),
    english={"text": "And as it seems to me, at precisely the same moment of time at which the light was visibly and bodily divided from the darkness, invisibly also the good angels were distinguished from those evil ones falling into the darkness of sin, these being turned to the light of justice and illuminated by the light, so that they should be light and not darkness. For thus the patterns of God's works had to agree, that the outcome of invisible things should follow the works of wisdom which were visible, according as that same wisdom which was working in both places here exercised judgement and there formed the pattern. The light, then, was divided from the darkness; and there was visible light and there were visible darknesses; likewise there was invisible light, and there were invisible darknesses; and a division of both was made, and the division of the darknesses and of the light was made at once. And the light was called day and the darkness night. God indeed divided both, and named both; but he did not make both. For God is not the author of darkness but of light; because sin is darkness and sin is nothing: And without him was made nothing (John 1:3). Light was made by him; because justice and truth are light, and of light we have heard what God said: Let there be light, and at once light was made (Gen 1). Nowhere did God say: Let there be darkness. But of light he said: Let there be light, and he divided the light from the darkness; and he called the light day, and the darkness night. Therefore he did not make both; yet he divided both, and named both. He divided by judgement; and by merit he named, judged and disposed. For since not even that could be disordered in his judging which was disordered among the offenders, he called the light day, and the darkness night.", **APPROVED},
    cruxes=["good-and-separated"], senses=["literal", "allegorical"],
    answers=["tenebrae-angeli", "tenebrae-privatio", "divisio-est-judicium", "divisio-est-instantanea"],
    notes="The most carefully joined statement of the Latin answer, and the join is the point. Hugh will not choose between the physical and the angelic reading: both happened, and they happened at the same moment of time, because the visible works of wisdom and the invisible outcomes had to match — 'here it exercised judgement, there it formed the pattern'. So there was visible light and visible darkness, invisible light and invisible darkness, and one division of both at once. Then he states Augustine's doctrine in its sharpest Latin — God divided both and named both, but did not make both, for sin is darkness and sin is nothing — and closes with the distinction the crux has been waiting for: he divided by judgement and named by merit. That the naming is by merit is Hugh's own, and it answers the question the rabbinic bench answers with R. Elazar's 'he never joins his name to the bad': what a thing is called follows what it deserves.")

add(id="hugh-sacr-1-4b", work="hugh-sacr", author="hugh-of-st-victor", tradition="latin",
    date=1134, date_precision="range-1130-1137", place="paris",
    anchor={"verse": "gen.1.4"}, lemma={"la": "per lucem ergo probavit lucem", "en": "by light, therefore, he proved light"},
    original=latin("11082", "CAP. XIII. Quare dicit Scriptura: « Vidit Deus lucem. »",
                   "quia in luce incommutabili vidit quod lux ista commutabilis suo modo et ordine bona esset.", 176),
    english={"text": "CHAPTER XIII. Why Scripture says: 'God saw the light.' But perhaps it occurs to someone: how did God see the light, that it was good, and divide the light from the darkness, and call the light day and the darkness night (Gen 1)? For if he saw his other works in the light, that they were good, in what light did he see the light, that it was good, and divide the light from the darkness, and call the light day and the darkness night? For nothing can be seen without light; but neither are darknesses seen without light — how much more is light not seen without light? What, then, was that light through which God saw the light, that it was good, and divided the light from the darkness? Did he see by that same light that it was good, or by another light? But how could he have seen through it that it was good, if he had not had another light through which and in which he saw that light, that it was good? For if he saw that this light was good, and truly saw it, he saw it in something of whose goodness there could be no doubt. Therefore there was another light, uncreated, in which this created light was seen to be good; because it imitated that light which is always good. For in that light, since it alone is good, both evils and goods are seen; and not only are evils and goods seen in it, but evils are seen to be evil and goods to be good in it, for the reason that it is truly and supremely good. For what is seen to disagree with it is seen to be evil; and what is seen to be like it is seen to be good; and both are seen only in it and through it. By light, therefore, he proved light: because in the unchangeable light he saw that this changeable light was good in its own mode and order.", **APPROVED},
    cruxes=["good-and-separated"], senses=["literal"],
    answers=["bonum-approbatio"],
    notes="A schoolman's difficulty made out of the order of the clauses, and worth the card because it is the only place on this crux where the seeing is treated as a problem in its own right rather than as a manner of speaking. If God saw the other works in the light of day one, in what light did he see that light? He cannot have seen it by itself, since nothing sees itself; so there was another and unchangeable light, and in that light he saw that this changeable one was good 'in its own mode and order'. Ambrose had refused the question by saying the seeing is not an inspection, Alcuin by making it a persistence of pleasure, Ibn Ezra by saying it is in the thought. Hugh alone answers it by positing a second light — uncreated, in which this changeable light was seen to be good because it imitated one that is always good — and then draws out what that light is for: in it not only are goods and evils seen, but goods are seen to be good and evils to be evil. The answer costs him a second light and buys him a criterion.")

add(id="rupert-gen-1-4", work="rupert-gen", author="rupert", tradition="latin",
    date=1117, date_precision="range-1112-1117", place="liege",
    anchor={"verse": "gen.1.4"}, lemma={"la": "terribile Dei judicium super diabolum", "en": "the terrible judgement of God upon the devil"},
    original=latin("10873", "Notandum in primis quod dictum est: Vidit Deus lucem quod esset bona.",
                   "proprio vitio facti sunt daemones tenebrosi.", 167),
    english={"text": "It is to be noted first of all that it is said: God saw the light, that it was good. (For none is good but one, God — Matt 19:17.) Further, whoever is called good, whether man or angel, is rightly called good only in respect of the good God, since, as none is good but one, God, whoever is good, or is said to be good, is good only in so far as he partakes of the good God. Accordingly it is not said: God saw the earth, that it was good, or the luminaries, that they were good, because they are not capable of goodness. But it is said of each: God saw that it was good — that is, a good thing, good, I say, that is, a useful thing. For the rational creature alone is capable of the divine goodness, that is, of the Spirit of God; and therefore man or angel alone is to be called good absolutely, namely in so far as man or angel has been made a partaker of the good God. Therefore this very judgement by which God saw the light, that it was good, compels that light to be understood which is capable of the divine goodness: a rational light, an intellectual light, the shining dignity of the angelic creature. But how is it said, And God saw the light, that it was good? Had he not seen, before he made it, that to make it was good? Does not the evangelist say of that Word in which he said 'let it be', that what was made, in him was life (John 1:3–4)? Therefore what he saw, he weighed with a just judgement and reckoned worthy of his regard. For there follows: And he divided the light and the darkness. This division of light and darkness intimates that a terrible judgement of God was made upon the devil. Of which the psalmist says: You are terrible, and who shall resist you? From then your anger (Ps 76:8 [Vg 75:8]). […] For since the devil resisted — that is, willed to resist you — you were angry, and he could not resist; for the angel fell and was made a devil. This terrible judgement is intimated by the division of light and darkness. For what are those darknesses, unless all the angels of Satan with their prince? These darknesses, surely, God divided from the light, because he separated the evil angels from the good. He divided them, I say, by an unchangeable separation, so that neither can those who fell rise again, nor can those who, though they could have sinned, refused to fall by sinning, ever fall by sinning any more. […] But whence those darknesses came has already been said: namely, from where the aforesaid light came, thence came those darknesses also; for they were shining angels, and by their own vice were made darksome demons.", **APPROVED},
    cruxes=["good-and-separated"], senses=["allegorical"],
    answers=["tenebrae-angeli", "divisio-est-judicium", "bonum-approbatio"],
    notes="Rupert argues to the angelic reading from the word 'good' rather than assuming it, and the argument is his own. None is good but God; whatever else is called good is good by partaking of him; only a rational creature can partake of him — therefore only a rational creature can be called good absolutely, and the fact that the text says of this light that it was good, and not merely that it was a good thing, forces the light to be intellectual. That is why, he notes, it is not said of the earth or of the luminaries that they were good. Then the division becomes a sentence passed: 'a terrible judgement of God upon the devil', unchangeable in both directions, so that the fallen cannot rise and the standing can no longer fall. And the answer to where the darkness came from is the one answer that makes it neither a privation nor a creature: it came from where the light came from — they were shining angels and made themselves dark. Elided: two psalm citations expounded at length. Rupert reads divisit lucem et tenebras without a preposition, which is neither Jerome's text nor the Old Latin's.")

add(id="honorius-hex-1-4", work="honorius-hex", author="honorius", tradition="latin",
    date=1130, date_precision="circa", place="regensburg",
    anchor={"verse": "gen.1.4"}, lemma={"la": "duodecim horas super terram, et duodecim sub terra", "en": "twelve hours above the earth and twelve below"},
    original=latin("10991", "Et vidit Deus lucem, quod esset bona. Hoc est, rationali creaturae",
                   "totidemque super terram delitescerent.", 172),
    english={"text": "And God saw the light, that it was good. That is, he gave the rational creature to understand how good are all the things it discerns by the light. And he divided the light and the darkness: namely, so that the light should shine twelve hours above the earth and twelve below the earth; and the darkness, put to flight by the light, should lie hidden twelve hours below the earth and as many above it.", **APPROVED},
    cruxes=["good-and-separated"], senses=["literal"],
    answers=["divisio-loci"],
    notes="The literal answer taken as far as it will go: the division is a timetable. The light of the first day travels on a twelve-hour circuit above the earth and twelve below, and the darkness does the reverse, hiding below while the light is above. Bede's difference of place has become a mechanism with hours in it, and there is no privation anywhere in the sentence — the darkness is put to flight by the light and lies hidden, which are things only a body does. On the ki tov half Honorius takes the seeing out of God altogether: 'he gave the rational creature to understand how good are all the things it discerns by the light', so the goodness is what we see by the light, not what God saw in it. Nobody else on either bench makes the approval a gift to the beholder.")

add(id="comestor-hs-1-4", work="comestor-hs", author="comestor", tradition="latin",
    date=1170, date_precision="circa", place="paris",
    anchor={"verse": "gen.1.4"}, lemma={"la": "umbram ex objectione corporum luci", "en": "the shadow made by bodies set against the light"},
    original=latin("11575", "Et vidit Deus lucem, quod esset bona, id est quae placuerat",
                   "stantes lux, cadentes tenebrae dicti sunt.", 198),
    english={"text": "And God saw the light, that it was good — that is, which had pleased him in his presence, or in his foreknowledge, that it should be made; it pleased him in its being, that it should remain. Or figuratively, he saw, that is, he made to be seen. And he divided the light and the darkness; here the disposing begins. And yet he says something of creation too, as though he created the darkness together with the light — that is, the shadow made by bodies set against the light; and he divided the created things by distance of place, and by quality, so that they should never be together, but always claim opposite hemispheres in turn. Here too the division of the angels is understood: those who stood were called light, those who fell darkness.", **APPROVED},
    cruxes=["good-and-separated"], senses=["literal", "allegorical"],
    answers=["tenebrae-creatura", "divisio-loci", "tenebrae-angeli", "bonum-approbatio"],
    notes="The twelfth-century summary, and the sentence in it that nobody else on the Latin bench writes: 'as though he created the darkness together with the light — that is, the shadow made by bodies set against the light.' It is a physical account of what darkness is that makes it a real effect of a real cause and not a privation, and Comestor slips it in with quasi and moves on to Bede's distance of place and the angels, keeping all three answers as Angelomus did three hundred years earlier. On the approval he splits the moment cleanly in two — it pleased God in his foreknowledge that it should be made, and pleased him in its being that it should remain — which is Alcuin's answer with a distinction added, and then offers 'he saw, that is, he made to be seen' as a figurative alternative, which is Remigius'. The Historia scholastica is at its most useful here: not a position, a map.")

# ---------------------------------------------------------------- threads (K9)
THREADS = [
 E("t-k9-01", "aug-civ-11-20", "br-3-6c", "parallel", "The star thread of this crux, and both benches reach it by reading a silence in the syntax. Augustine: on day one 'and God saw that it was good' is placed before the division and not after it, 'lest he should seem to have borne the testimony of his approval to such darkness along with the light' — and on day four, where the darkness is blameless, it is placed after. R. Elazar: 'the Holy One never joins his name to the bad but only to the good, for it is not written and to the darkness GOD called night, but and to the darkness he called night.' One argues from where a clause of approval stands, the other from where the divine name is dropped, at consecutive verses, with no contact of any kind."),
 E("t-k9-02", "aug-civ-11-20", "br-2-5", "parallel", "The same inference from the same omission at the same clause. R. Abahu: 'but I do not know which of them he desires — since it is written and God saw the light that it was good, he desires the deeds of the righteous and does not desire the deeds of the wicked.' Augustine: 'lest both should be called good, when one of them was evil.' Both take the absence of an approval of the darkness to be a deliberate withholding, and both make it decisive."),
 E("t-k9-03", "br-3-8-yanai", "br-2-5", "echoes", "The same reading twice in the same midrash, once as an argument and once as a mapping. R. Abahu at 2:5 states the difficulty ('I do not know which of them he desires') and resolves it from Gen 1:4; R. Yanai at 3:8 runs the same identifications through every clause of 1:2–1:5 without raising a difficulty at all. The mapping is identical: tohu the deeds of the wicked, 'let there be light' the deeds of the righteous."),
 E("t-k9-04", "isidore-quaest-1-4", "br-3-8-yanai", "parallel", "The moral reading on both benches at the same clause, and the difference between them is exact. R. Yanai: 'and God divided between the light and the darkness — between the deeds of the righteous and the deeds of the wicked.' Isidore: 'then God, according to the favour of his foreknowledge, divided the just, that is, the sons of God and of light, from sinners, as from darkness; calling the just day and those others night.' The rabbinic bench divides deeds, the Latin bench divides people. Neither ever discusses the difference, and it holds across every allegory on this verse."),
 E("t-k9-05", "aug-gnl-imp-5-25", "br-3-8-yanai", "parallel", "Augustine reaches the righteous and the wicked at this clause too, and gets there by a route the rabbinic bench does not need: God is not the author of our vices but is their orderer, when he sets sinners in the place they deserve — sheep on the right, goats on the left. Because he has made the darkness a privation he cannot let the wicked be the darkness outright, only be ordered under it; R. Yanai, under no such constraint, simply says the darkness is their deeds."),
 E("t-k9-06", "aug-gnm-1-9", "aug-gnl-imp-5-23", "echoes", "Four years apart, the same doctrine argued from two different angles: at De Genesi contra Manichaeos from the analogy of silence, which explains how Scripture can say God makes darkness; at De Genesi ad litteram imperfectus from the analogy of the garment and nakedness, which explains why no separate act of dividing was needed."),
 E("t-k9-07", "aug-gnl-imp-5-25", "aug-gnm-1-9", "echoes", "The privation doctrine completed. The earlier work says God does not make the darkness but is rightly said to make it by withholding light; the later adds the term that the whole tradition will use — he does not make it, but he orders it, 'lest even those privations should not have their own order' — and supplies the rests in music and the shadows in painting to show what ordering a nothing can mean."),
 E("t-k9-08", "aug-civ-11-19", "aug-gnl-imp-5-25", "contests", "Augustine against Augustine, and he knows it: the earlier work takes the light of day one as possibly ethereal, sensitive or rational and the darkness as the privation of whichever it is; the City of God offers the reading in which the light is the angels and the darkness the apostate spirits, and hedges it — 'it does not seem to me an opinion at odds with the works of God'. Every later Latin witness on this crux has to hold both, and Hugh's solution is to say that both divisions happened at the same instant."),
 E("t-k9-09", "wigbod-gen-1-4", "aug-civ-11-20", "cites", "'Et cur non dicit Scriptura, vidit Deus tenebras quod essent bonae, sicut de luce dictum est? … in his dicens quia boni sunt, in illis nullatenus dicens, ne peccata quae erant tenebrae adprobare videretur' reproduces City of God XI.20's argument and its reason almost word for word, under the rubric AUGUSTINUS — on the same page as the proposition that there is an essence of darkness, which Augustine denies everywhere."),
 E("t-k9-10", "wigbod-gen-1-4", "aug-gnm-1-9", "contests", "The sharpest disagreement on the Latin bench, and it is invisible because it is filed under Augustine's name. Augustine: Scripture does not say God made the darkness, because darkness is the absence of light. Wigbod's pupil: everything that is divided can also be, so as there is an essence of light, so there is an essence of darkness. The inference is valid on its own terms and it destroys the doctrine the same compilation is transmitting."),
 E("t-k9-11", "angelom-gen-1-4", "aug-gnm-1-9", "cites", "The silence analogy copied close enough to be certain — clamando vocem, silentium autem non clamando, cessatio vocis silentium est — with one word altered: Augustine writes that God makes darkness because he 'either does not give light or withdraws it', Angelomus that he 'either gives or withdraws light'. The change turns a withholding into a conferring, and Angelomus names Augustine two sentences later as the authority he is following."),
 E("t-k9-12", "angelom-gen-1-4", "bede-gen-1-4", "cites", "'Lucem videlicet in superiore orbis parte diffundendo, in qua humana erat conversatio futura, inferiora vero ejus priscis in tenebris remanendo' is Bede's sentence with one verb changed, introduced by 'as Clement says: lighting up one part of the world, he left the other dark.' Angelomus has the words from Bede and the attribution from somewhere else."),
 E("t-k9-13", "rabanus-gen-1-4", "bede-gen-1-4", "transmits", "Verbatim and unnamed, from 'Divisit namque eas non solum qualitatis, sed et locorum distantia' to the end of the sentence. The transmission is what makes 'by quality and by place' the standing literal answer of the Latin bench."),
 E("t-k9-14", "comestor-hs-1-4", "bede-gen-1-4", "echoes", "'Et creatas divisit locorum distantia, et qualitate' is Bede's pair of terms four centuries on, with the mechanism supplied — 'so that they should never be together, but always claim opposite hemispheres in turn' — and with the darkness made a created shadow rather than an ancient dark left in place."),
 E("t-k9-15", "honorius-hex-1-4", "bede-gen-1-4", "echoes", "The same answer turned into a timetable: twelve hours above the earth and twelve below, the darkness put to flight and lying hidden. Bede's 'difference of places' has become a circuit with a count of hours, and the darkness has become the kind of thing that can be driven off and can hide."),
 E("t-k9-16", "basil-hex-2-8", "bede-gen-1-4", "parallel", "Both answer 'why divide them?' with distance, and neither could have got it from the other in this form: Basil, that God fashioned their natures unmingled and set them apart from one another by long spaces; Bede, that he divided them not only by quality but by place, the light over the upper part of the world. Basil's Latin was available in Northumbria, but the shared term is the commonplace, not a phrase."),
 E("t-k9-17", "remigius-gen-1-4", "aug-gnm-1-9", "contests", "The flattest contradiction of Augustine on this crux, without naming him and without argument from authority: 'God made both, the light and the darkness … therefore the darkness too is a work of God', proved from the Benedicite, where light and darkness are called together to bless the Lord (Dan 3:72). If darkness is the absence of light it cannot be summoned to praise; the canticle summons it."),
 E("t-k9-18", "glossa-1-4", "remigius-gen-1-4", "cites", "The Glossa's whole literal gloss on this verse is Remigius' sentence with its proof text — 'Lucem et tenebras fecit Deus, unde Dan. III: Benedicite, lux et tenebrae, Domino, cum omnis creatura ad benedicendum invitatur' — printed under the name of Strabo. What the twelfth-century schoolroom reads on Gen 1:4 is therefore the anti-Augustinian answer."),
 E("t-k9-19", "glossa-1-4", "isidore-quaest-1-4", "cites", "'Habet quisque in operibus et recta vita, distinctos sex dies, post quos requiem speret: primo die, lucem fidei' is Isidore's six ages of the Christian life, named as such, reduced to its first day. The Glossa keeps the moral reading and drops the division of the just from sinners that was its content."),
 E("t-k9-20", "remigius-gen-1-4b", "isidore-quaest-1-4", "echoes", "The same six-day allegory carried from the individual life to the history of the world: Isidore's first day is the light of faith and the dividing of the just from sinners, Remigius' is the separation of the sons of Seth from the generation of Cain, with the flood as its evening. The scheme is Isidore's, the datable content is not."),
 E("t-k9-21", "remigius-gen-1-4b", "remigius-gen-1-4", "contests", "One commentator, one verse, two incompatible readings a few columns apart, and no attempt to reconcile them: on the literal pass the darkness is a work of God made so that men might rest, and on the allegorical pass the darkness is the impious line of Cain that God drowned. The Latin bench's habit of running the six days twice is what allows the same writer to hold both."),
 E("t-k9-22", "hugh-sacr-1-4", "aug-civ-11-19", "echoes", "Hugh takes over the angelic division and removes Augustine's hedge, then adds what Augustine does not say: that the visible division and the invisible one happened at precisely the same moment of time, because the visible works of wisdom and the invisible outcomes had to correspond."),
 E("t-k9-23", "hugh-sacr-1-4", "aug-gnl-imp-5-23", "echoes", "The instantaneity argument transferred. Augustine: no separate act of separation was needed, because the division followed by the very fact that the light was made. Hugh: the division of the darknesses and of the light was made at once — and applies it to the angels, so that the fall and the confirmation are one instant with the making of light."),
 E("t-k9-24", "hugh-sacr-1-4", "br-3-6c", "parallel", "'He divided by judgement, and by merit he named' and R. Elazar's 'the Holy One never joins his name to the bad but only to the good' are the same doctrine of naming: what a thing is called follows what it deserves, and the naming is therefore a second act after the judging, not a label. Hugh gets it from the order divisit … vocavit, R. Elazar from a missing subject in the second half of the verse."),
 E("t-k9-25", "rupert-gen-1-4", "aug-civ-11-19", "echoes", "The angelic reading with a new argument under it. Augustine offers it as one true reading among several; Rupert derives it from the word 'good' — none is good but God, only a rational creature can partake of him, therefore the light of which it is said 'that it was good' must be intellectual — and then makes the division a sentence pronounced on the devil, unchangeable in both directions."),
 E("t-k9-26", "rupert-gen-1-4", "aug-civ-11-20", "contests", "Both build on the placing of 'God saw that it was good', and they build opposite things. Augustine: the approval is put before the division so that God should not seem to approve the darkness. Rupert: the approval is put where it is because it is a judgement passed on a rational light — and the division that follows is the judgement passed on the rest. Where Augustine finds a refusal to approve, Rupert finds a verdict delivered."),
 E("t-k9-27", "hugh-sacr-1-4b", "ambrose-hex-1-9b", "parallel", "The same difficulty and two refusals to let it become a problem about God's knowledge. Ambrose: 'he did not see what he did not know; nor did he approve what he had not known before' — the seeing is the self-attestation of a good work. Hugh takes the question seriously and answers it with a second light: in the unchangeable light he saw that this changeable light was good in its own mode and order."),
 E("t-k9-28", "hugh-sacr-1-4b", "alcuin-int-35", "echoes", "Alcuin dissolves the difficulty — what pleased him to be made pleased him to remain — and Hugh reopens it as a question about the medium of the seeing. Both are answering the objection that if God saw the light was good he learned something; the Carolingian schoolbook answers in one sentence what the Paris schoolroom takes a chapter over."),
 E("t-k9-29", "comestor-hs-1-4", "alcuin-int-35", "echoes", "'Quae placuerat in praesentia, vel praescientia, ut fieret; placuit in essentia, ut maneret' is Alcuin's answer with the moment divided in two, foreknowledge and being; the doubling is Comestor's and the pattern — what pleased to be made pleased to remain — is Alcuin's."),
 E("t-k9-30", "ibn-ezra-1-4", "aug-gnm-1-9", "parallel", "Both come to the possibility that the dividing and the naming are one act, and only one of them takes it. Augustine: 'perhaps, then, the dividing between light and darkness just is the calling of the light day and the darkness night, so that to have ordered these is to have named them' — and he leaves it as a perhaps and goes on. Ibn Ezra states it flatly as the meaning of the word: 'and the sense of and he divided is by the calling of names.' Nothing else on the Latin bench even entertains it."),
 E("t-k9-31", "ibn-ezra-1-4", "ambrose-hex-1-9b", "parallel", "The seeing removed from the world in two ways. Ibn Ezra: 'and he saw' is like Qohelet's 'I myself saw' — it is in the thought, so no act is reported. Ambrose: God did not see what he did not know, and the seeing is the good work attesting itself. Both refuse to let the verse describe an inspection, and neither has to posit anything to do it, which is what separates them from Hugh's second light."),
 E("t-k9-32", "ramban-1-2-choshech", "comestor-hs-1-4", "parallel", "The two most physical accounts of what the darkness is, from benches that cannot borrow. Ramban: the darkness is elemental fire, which is dark, 'and were it red, it would redden the night for us'. Comestor: God created the darkness with the light, that is, the shadow made by bodies set against the light. Both make the darkness a real thing with a cause, and Comestor has to introduce his with quasi because Augustine stands behind him; Ramban states his as the plain sense."),
 E("t-k9-33", "pdre-3-5", "remigius-gen-1-4", "parallel", "Darkness as a creature, stated without argument on both benches. Pirkei de-Rabbi Eliezer puts it fourth in a list of eight things created on the first day, between light and tohu, with no proof text and no defence. Remigius says God made both and proves it from the Benedicite. Neither is arguing with anybody; each is the settled answer of a tradition, and they are the same answer."),
 E("t-k9-34", "pdre-3-5", "alcuin-int-35", "parallel", "Two enumerations of what was made at the beginning, answering the same question with incompatible inventories. Alcuin, at the question next but one to this: heaven, earth, angels, light, air, water and the soul of man. Pirkei de-Rabbi Eliezer: heaven, earth, light, darkness, tohu, bohu, wind and water. The Latin list has the angels and the soul and no darkness; the rabbinic list has the darkness and the tohu and no angels. Neither list argues, and between them they contain the whole disagreement of this crux."),
 E("t-k9-35", "abelard-hex-1-4", "wigbod-gen-1-4", "parallel", "'Distinctio rei formatae ab informi', given as a third answer in a Carolingian dialogue, and 'operatione sua perfectum discrevit ab imperfecto et distinctum a confuso', argued at the Paraclete three hundred years later to prove that the creator of the matter and the former of the works are one and the same. The same reading of the darkness — not a privation, not a creature, but the unordered state of the world — put to two entirely different uses."),
 E("t-k9-36", "abelard-hex-1-4", "aug-gnl-imp-5-25", "contests", "Both make the division an ordering rather than a making, and they mean opposite things by it. Augustine orders privations, so that even a nothing has its place. Abelard orders works, so that the distinction of the works just is the light and the confusion before them is the darkness — which leaves nothing for the privation doctrine to be about."),
 E("t-k9-37", "vulgate-1-4", "lxx-1-4", "transmits", "The Greek keeps the Hebrew's doubled preposition, ana meson tou photos kai ana meson tou skotous, and the Old Latin behind Augustine keeps it too: divisit inter lucem et tenebras. Jerome writes divisit lucem a tenebris. Which form a Latin witness quotes tracks his text closely enough to be visible on this daf: Augustine, Angelomus and Alcuin have the older form, Bede, Remigius, the Glossa and Comestor Jerome's, and Rupert has neither."),
 E("t-k9-38", "targ-psj-1-4", "targ-onk-1-4", "echoes", "Pseudo-Jonathan reproduces Onkelos word for word at Gen 1:4 and expands freely at Gen 1:5 — the light made so that the inhabitants of the world might labour by it, the darkness so that the creatures might rest in it. The expansive targum finds nothing to expand in the dividing, which is the whole of the Latin bench's difficulty."),
 E("t-k9-39", "remigius-gen-1-4", "targ-psj-1-4", "parallel", "The same reason for the division, given in Aramaic and in Latin with no possible contact: Pseudo-Jonathan, that God made the day so that the inhabitants of the world might labour by it and the night so that the creatures might rest in it; Remigius, that God made both the light and the darkness 'that men might work in the light and rest in the night'. Remigius uses it to prove the darkness is a creature; the targum draws no conclusion from it at all."),
 E("t-k9-40", "br-3-6b", "bede-gen-1-4", "parallel", "R. Yoḥanan and Resh Lakish's king with two generals, each claiming the day, told 'day shall be your bound' and 'night shall be your bound', and Bede's division 'not only by quality but by place', with the light over the upper part of the world and the lower left dark. Both make va-yavdel an assignment of territory, and the midrash says outright what Bede assumes: havdala mamash, a real separating and not a figure."),
 E("t-k9-41", "br-3-6b", "aug-gnl-imp-5-25", "contests", "The two benches at their furthest apart on the same clause. Augustine: God did not make the darkness, he ordered it, as a singer orders his silences. R. Tanḥuma, on Isa 45:7, 'who forms light and creates darkness, who makes peace': after they were created he made peace between them — the darkness is created, the making of peace is the dividing, and both are works. The rabbinic text has no term for a privation and never needs one."),
 E("t-k9-42", "rupert-gen-1-4", "br-3-6b", "parallel", "The division as a judicial sentence, on both benches, with the same insistence that it is irreversible. Rupert: a terrible judgement of God upon the devil, 'by an unchangeable separation, so that neither can those who fell rise again, nor can those who refused to fall ever fall'. The midrash: the king calls each general and fixes his bound, and R. Berekhya introduces it as havdala mamash. The Latin makes it a verdict on persons and the rabbinic a decree about times, and both are about what may never be crossed again."),
 E("t-k9-43", "ibn-ezra-1-4", "br-3-6b", "contests", "Ibn Ezra's 'the sense of and he divided is by the calling of names' is the flat denial of R. Yoḥanan and Resh Lakish's havdala mamash, a real separating, and he does not name them. He is also refusing R. Ze'eira's derivation, since a naming cannot be the source of a rule about looking at a flame. The grammarian dismantles in one clause the two things the midrash builds on this word."),
 E("t-k9-44", "aug-civ-11-19", "br-3-6b", "contests", "Both benches read the darkness of this verse as something set aside, and the thing set aside is not the same. For Augustine it is the apostate angels, ordered but not approved; for R. Yehuda bar Simon and the Rabbis it is the light that is set aside — hivdilo lo, he separated it for himself, or for the righteous in the time to come. One tradition takes the division to be a removal of the darkness from fellowship, the other a reservation of the light from use, and each is unavailable to the other."),
 E("t-k9-45", "glossa-1-4", "aug-gnm-1-9", "contests", "The Glossa's own gloss on Gen 1:2, three columns earlier, is 'and there were darknesses, namely the privation of true light'; its gloss on Gen 1:4 is 'God made both the light and the darkness'. The standard schoolbook prints Augustine's doctrine on one verse and its contradiction on the next, and marks neither as disputed."),
 E("t-k9-x1", "rashi-1-4", "br-3-6b", "cites", "Phase 4. Rashi's plain sense names Bereshit Rabbah 3:6 for it: 'he saw that it was good, and that it was not seemly that light and darkness should function together in a confused manner. He therefore limited this one's sphere of activity to the daytime, and this one's to the nighttime' — which is R. Yoḥanan and Resh Lakish's king with two generals, each told his bound. Written at Phase 4 because `rashi-1-4` was built for K8 and carries both cruxes' answers in one dibbur."),
 E("t-k9-x2", "rashi-1-4", "b-chag-12a-light", "cites", "Phase 4. Rashi's aggadic half names Chagigah 12a: 'he saw that the wicked were unworthy of using it; he therefore set it apart, reserving it for the righteous in the world to come'. The dibbur gives its two sources in order, the aggadah first and the peshat second, and each belongs to a different crux on this site."),
 E("t-k9-x3", "rashi-1-4", "ambrose-hex-1-9b", "parallel", "Phase 4. The same purpose clause in Troyes and in Milan, seven hundred years apart. Rashi: it was not seemly that light and darkness should function together in a confused manner. Ambrose: the distinction is made between the light and the darkness so that, their natures being separated, nothing may be seen to have anything confused within it. Neither could have known the other, and it is the closest verbal agreement between the benches on this crux."),
 E("t-k9-x4", "b-chag-12a-light", "br-3-6b", "parallel", "Phase 4. Two redactions of one answer to va-yavdel: the Bavli that the light was stored away for the righteous, and Bereshit Rabbah's Rabbis that 'he separated it for the righteous in the time to come, like a king who had a fine portion and set it aside for his son' — beside R. Yehuda bar Simon's harder reading, that he separated it for himself."),
]

FINDING = (
 "Both benches decide this crux by reading a silence, and they find the same silence at consecutive clauses of "
 "the same verse. Augustine notices that on the first day 'and God saw that it was good' stands before the "
 "division and not after it, whereas on the fourth day, where the darkness is blameless, it stands after — and "
 "concludes that the placing is deliberate: God does not repeat his approval once the darkness is in view, "
 "'lest both should be called good, when one of them was evil'. Bereshit Rabbah reads the next clause the same "
 "way and gets the same answer: it is not written 'and to the darkness God called night' but 'and to the "
 "darkness he called night', because the Holy One never joins his name to the bad but only to the good. And R. "
 "Abahu, at Bereshit Rabbah 2:5, argues from the identical omission at the identical clause that God desires "
 "the deeds of the righteous and not those of the wicked. Three arguments from what the verse declines to say, "
 "on two benches, with no contact — and it is the only place on this site where the two traditions use the same "
 "technique on the same words to reach the same conclusion.\n\n"
 "What they do not share is the question underneath. The Latin bench cannot get past whether darkness is "
 "anything, because Augustine settled that it is not — it is the absence of light, as silence is the ceasing of "
 "voice and nakedness the absence of a garment — and then had to explain how a nothing can be divided, which he "
 "did with the finest thing said on this crux in either language: God did not make the darkness, he ordered it, "
 "as a singer orders the rests in his song and a painter the shadows that set off what is prominent. Every "
 "Latin witness after him is placed by that doctrine, including the ones who reject it. And they do reject it, "
 "in the schoolroom rather than in the controversy: Wigbod's dialogue puts under the rubric AUGUSTINUS the "
 "inference that everything divided can also be, so that as there is an essence of light there is an essence of "
 "darkness; Remigius says God made both and proves it from the Benedicite, where light and darkness are called "
 "together to bless the Lord, which a privation cannot be; and the Glossa prints Remigius' sentence as the "
 "standard gloss on Gen 1:4 three columns after printing Augustine's privation as the standard gloss on Gen "
 "1:2. The twelfth century then argues from Augustine's doctrine on a page that no longer teaches it.\n\n"
 "The rabbinic bench never asks the question at all, and its answers are correspondingly unembarrassed. "
 "Pirkei de-Rabbi Eliezer lists darkness fourth among the eight things created on the first day, with no proof "
 "text and no defence. R. Tanḥuma quotes Isaiah's 'who forms light and creates darkness, who makes peace' and "
 "says that after they were created he made peace between them. Ramban says the darkness is the element of "
 "fire, which is dark, and that were it red it would redden the night for us. Comestor, who knows perfectly "
 "well that shadow is what bodies cast, has to introduce the same thought with a quasi. What the rabbinic bench "
 "has instead of the metaphysical question is a liturgical one: R. Ze'eira derives from 'he saw … and he "
 "divided' the rule that one does not bless the lamp at havdala until he has used its light, so that the verse "
 "ends as the source of a benediction still said every week — and nothing on the Latin bench has that shape, "
 "because nothing on the Latin bench is looking for a practice."
)

